module lib/gpt/story_gpt  documents every variable
  A small GPT learns one line of text and then writes it. The text is
  "hello world! " five times over, so every character is fixed by the two
  before it: after "l" can come "l", "o" or "d", and only the character
  before the "l" says which. A model that sees one character at a time
  cannot learn that; the model has to look back, and attention is how it
  looks back.

<main>  @9b4a6e4a
  what            Train a GPT with two blocks of two heads on the line, thirty
                  steps of four windows each, then give it the prompt "hello" and
                  let it write. It has 6,560 parameters and a vocabulary of the 9
                  characters of the line
                  [test "the model has 6,560 parameters, and 9 characters make its vocabulary"].
                  The loss starts at $\log 9$, the loss of a uniform guess
                  [test "the first loss is log 9 exactly, because the output map starts at zero"],
                  and ends at 0.1991
                  [test "thirty steps take the loss from 2.197 to 0.1991"];
                  from the prompt the model writes the line on, character by
                  character [test "from the prompt hello, the model writes the line on"].
  TEXT         the training text
  PROMPT       the text the model is asked to continue
  STEPS        training steps
  SAMPLES      characters to write after the prompt
  INIT_SEED    the seed of the starting parameters
  SAMPLE_SEED  the seed of the draws when writing
  vocab        the distinct characters of the text, in code order
  tokens       the text as tokens: each character's place in vocab
  cfg          the model's sizes: at most 8 characters read at once, 16
               numbers for a character inside the model, 2 attention heads
               per block, 2 blocks, an MLP inner width of 64 (four times
               the width, as in GPT-2), and 4 windows of text per training step
  model        the model: its matrices, a record of them, trained in place
  count        how many parameters the model has
  losses       the loss of each training step's batch
  written      the prompt and the characters the model wrote after it, as text

  tokenize  list the characters the text uses and turn the text into their indices
  build     size the model and draw its matrices' starting values
    run       The model has {count} parameters.
  train     train it on windows of the text
  generate  continue the prompt one drawn character at a time, and turn the tokens back into text
    run       The model wrote: {written}

path core  How a GPT learns to continue a line
  <main>                         The task: a line of text whose characters depend on the ones before
    > The model reads up to eight characters and predicts the next one at every position. Before training it knows nothing, and its output map starts at zero, so its first prediction is uniform over the nine characters and its loss is $\log 9$ [test "the first loss is log 9 exactly, because the output map starts at zero"].
  gpt_forward/embed              Characters become rows of numbers
    > Each character becomes its row of the token embedding plus its position's row of the position embedding. The sequence is now a matrix with one row per position: the residual stream.
  block                          A block: attention, then the MLP, each added to the stream
  attention/scores               Each position scores every position by query against key
  attention/mask                 The mask: no position may look ahead
    > Without the mask, position $i$ could read position $i + 1$, the character it is being trained to predict, and would learn to copy it. The mask sets those scores so that their weights are exactly zero.
  attention/weights              A softmax turns each row of scores into weights
  attention/mix                  Each position's output is a weighted average of the values
  block/mlp                      The MLP works on each position alone
  sequence_loss/loss             Cross-entropy scores the prediction at every position
  train/forward_backward         The gradient: the forward pass, run backward
    > `value_and_grad` runs each window's forward pass and then the backward pass the compiler wrote for it. Backpropagation starts at the loss: the gradient with respect to the logits is the predicted distribution with one taken off the right character, and from there the gradient goes back through every step of the forward pass in reverse order, to the rows of the embeddings the window looked up.
  optimizer_step/muon            Muon: momentum, orthogonalized, for the blocks' matrices
  optimizer_step/adam            Adam for the embeddings and the output map
  train                          Thirty steps: the loss falls from a guess toward zero
    > The loss is measured on the batch each step trains on, so it jumps around: windows that start at different characters are not equally easy. It ends at 0.1991 [test "thirty steps take the loss from 2.197 to 0.1991"].
  sample/draw                    Writing: draw a character, append it, repeat
    > Each new character is drawn from the model's probabilities given the last eight characters written. From "hello" the model writes the line on [test "from the prompt hello, the model writes the line on"].
